==English==
[[File:Pierogi.jpg|thumb|A plate of '''pierogi'''.]]

===Alternative forms===
* {{l|en|pirogi}}, {{l|en|perogie}}, {{l|en|perogi}}, {{l|en|pirogie}}, {{l|en|pyrogy}}, {{l|en|pyrohy}}, {{l|en|perogy}}

===Etymology===
Borrowed from {{bor|en|pl|pierogi}}, the plural of {{m|pl|pieróg||dumpling}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/pəˈɹoːɡi/|lang=en}}, {{IPA|/pɛˈɾoːɡi/|lang=en}} (amongst Polish Americans)
* {{audio|en-ne-pierogi.ogg|Audio|lang=en}}
* {{hyphenation|pier|ogi|lang=en}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|pierogi|pierogies|pierogis}}

# {{lb|en|North America}} A [[square]]- or [[crescent]]-shaped [[dumpling]] of [[unleavened]] [[dough]], [[stuff]]ed with [[sauerkraut]], [[cheese]], [[mash]]ed [[potatoes]], [[cabbage]], [[onion]], [[meat]], or any combination of these, or with a [[fruit]] [[filling]].

====Usage notes====
* Russian "[[pirozhki]]" ({{l|ru||пирожки́}}) and "[[pirogi]]" (singular "[[pirog]]", {{cog|ru||пироги́}}) and Polish [[pierogi]] (diminutive: "pierożki") (dumplings) are often confused. The two dishes are quite different. See [[pelmeni]] for the Russian version of the Polish [[pierogi]]. In certain regions of the Ukraine, these terms (пироги́, пиріжки́) may refer to either the Polish "pierogi" or the Russian "pirozhki".

